  "id": 427601181,
  "iid": 22107,
  "project_id": 7898047,
  "title": "wsutil: Define JSMN_PARENT_LINKS to accelerate JSON parsing",
  "description": "**Summary:**\nThis MR defines JSMN_PARENT_LINKS to greatly improve JSON parsing performance improvements and expand the largest NetLog file allowed, since JSON parsing is no longer expected to be a bottleneck.\n\n**Details:**\nwsutil uses JSMN as the underlying JSON parsing library. Currently, parsing large files with JSMN is very slow. On my i9 system, Wireshark took 33 seconds to load a 100 MB netlog (JSON) file.\n\nAccording to JSMN's developer:\n\n> In the benchmark jsmn have shown pretty good results for small objects (~4KB). But for large objects it was terribly slow. This can be fixed by storing links to the parent nodes. You will loose about 4 bytes per token, but speed will be much higher (by the way, after this little hack jsmn seems to be the fastest parser in that benchmark).\n> \n> To enable parent links you should define `JSMN_PARENT_LINKS` before compiling jsmn.\n\nI retested before/after defining `JSMN_PARENT_LINKS` on a debug build with a 200 MB file and saw a significant improvement:\n\nCommand: `/usr/bin/time -v \"./run/tshark\" -r \"chrome-net-export-log_200mb.json\" > /dev/null`\n\nWithout JSMN_PARENT_LINKS defined (current):\n```\nUser time (seconds): 1043.75\nSystem time (seconds): 1.09\nMaximum resident set size (kbytes): 437376\n```\n\nWith JSMN_PARENT_LINKS defined:\n```\nUser time (seconds): 0.98\nSystem time (seconds): 0.72\nMaximum resident set size (kbytes): 456960\n```\n\nThis seems to have resulted in about a 4.9% memory increase (19584/437376), but a 99.8% time reduction (1043.14/1044.84), which should be an acceptable tradeoff.\n\nThis MR defines JSMN_PARENT_LINKS to realize those performance improvements and expand the largest NetLog file allowed, since JSON parsing is no longer expected to be a bottleneck.\n\nReferences:\n* https://zserge.com/jsmn/\n* https://github.com/jkuhlmann/cgltf/issues/66",
